= Validation set =

== Overview ==
A '''validation set''' is data excluded from direct model-parameter fitting and used during development to choose models, hyperparameters, thresholds, stopping points, preprocessing decisions, or other design choices.

Validation estimates guide development.

== Model selection ==
A validation set can be used to compare:

* architectures;
* regularization strengths;
* feature sets;
* hyperparameters;
* early-stopping checkpoints;
* decision thresholds.

Because those choices depend on validation performance, the final selected system is indirectly adapted to the validation set.

== Validation overfitting ==
Repeated experimentation can overfit the validation criterion.

If many alternatives are tried, the best observed validation score may partly reflect noise.

This is one source of [[Model selection bias]].

== Validation and testing ==
A [[Test set]] should remain independent of the development choices being evaluated.

If test results are repeatedly used to change the system, the test set has become another validation set in practice.

== Cross-validation ==
Cross-validation estimates development performance by rotating data through training and validation roles.

Nested cross-validation can separate inner model selection from outer performance estimation.

== Related concepts ==
[[Training set]], [[Test set]], [[Overfitting]], [[Model selection bias]], [[Validation]], and [[Data leakage (machine learning)]] provide the surrounding evaluation context.

== Model-selection feedback ==
A validation set is not a pristine test set after it has been used repeatedly to select hyperparameters, prompts or thresholds. The development procedure may optimize to random variation in its scores. It can still be useful for guiding training, but the selected score is not an unbiased final estimate of generalization.

== Nested evaluation ==
Under nested cross-validation, inner folds guide model selection while outer folds estimate performance of that selection procedure. The outer-fold data must not influence the inner selection of a corresponding fitted model. This offers a more defensible assessment than choosing the best configuration with the same cross-validation score used as the final estimate.

== Limits of nesting ==
Nested cross-validation itself relies on representative sampling, valid group/time partitions, unbiased labels and correctly scoped preprocessing. Repeating experiments until an outer result looks favorable can again make that outer result part of model selection; a genuinely independent final test remains valuable where feasible.

